    <h1>How to Combine Multiple Images into One PDF (Free, 2025)</h1>
    <p class="meta">6 min read</p>

    <p>Sooner or later, almost everyone needs to combine images into a PDF. Maybe you took photos of a scanned document, and each page is a separate picture. Maybe you have a pile of receipts to send for a refund. Or you may need to put your ID, resume, and certificates into one file for a job application. Even a photo album is easier to share as a single PDF than as fifty loose pictures.</p>
    <p>The good news is that it is easy, and it does not cost anything. In this guide, you will learn three free ways to merge JPG into PDF and turn multiple images into one PDF. Two methods use tools already on your computer. The third works in any web browser.</p>

    <h2>Method 1: Combine Images on Windows (Built-in)</h2>
    <p>Windows has a hidden tool called <strong>Microsoft Print to PDF</strong>. It works like a printer, but instead of using paper, it saves a PDF file. You can use it to combine photos into a PDF without installing anything.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open <strong>File Explorer</strong> and go to the folder with your images.</li>
      <li>Hold the <strong>Ctrl</strong> key and click each image you want to include.</li>
      <li>Right-click one of the selected images and choose <strong>Print</strong>. On Windows 11, you may need to click <strong>Show more options</strong> first.</li>
      <li>In the Print Pictures window, set the printer to <strong>Microsoft Print to PDF</strong>.</li>
      <li>On the right, choose the <strong>Full page photo</strong> layout. Each image will get its own page.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Print</strong>, type a name for your file, and click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The Photos app uses the same Print window (press Ctrl+P), but File Explorer makes it easier to pick many images at once.</p>
    <p class="note"><strong>Tip:</strong> The pages usually follow the order of the file names. To control the order, rename your files 1, 2, 3, and so on before you print. Also, Windows does not give you much control over margins or page size.</p>

    <h2>Method 2: Combine Images on Mac (Preview App)</h2>
    <p>Every Mac comes with Preview, which can turn many pictures into one PDF. It also lets you drag pages into the order you want.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>In Finder, select all the images you want to combine.</li>
      <li>Right-click them, choose <strong>Open With</strong>, and then choose <strong>Preview</strong>. All the images open in one window.</li>
      <li>If you do not see the small pictures on the left, click <strong>View</strong> and choose <strong>Thumbnails</strong>.</li>
      <li>Drag the thumbnails up or down to put them in the right order.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>File</strong>, then <strong>Print</strong>. Press <strong>Command+A</strong> in the thumbnail bar if you want to be sure every page is included.</li>
      <li>Click the <strong>PDF</strong> menu at the bottom left and choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong>.</li>
      <li>Type a name, pick a folder, and click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This method works well, but it only works on a Mac. If you use both Windows and Mac, or you work on a phone or school computer, the next method is easier.</p>

